"Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1)\nciteturn30936view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 864\nL94:   19. cite84†I Emergent Misalignment Evaluation L95:     1. cite85†I.1 Experimental Setup L96:     2. cite86†I.2 Results L97:       1. cite87†Alignment pretraining does not mitigate EM. L98:       2. cite88†Validation of our evaluation suite. L99:   20. cite89†J Misalignment Evaluations L100:     1. cite90†J.1 Create Discourse-Grounded Evaluations L101:     2. cite91†J.2 Misalignment Benchmark Validation L102:     3. cite92†J.3 Generating Synthetic Pretraining Documents L103: cite93†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L104: \nL105: arXiv:2601.10160v1 [cs.CL] 15 Jan 2026\nL106: # Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment\nL107: \nL108: Cameron Tice Affiliation: Geodesic Research    Puria Radmard Affiliation: Geodesic Research Affiliation: University of Cambridge    Samuel Ratnam Affiliation: University of Oxford    Andy Kim Affiliation: Independent    David Africa Affiliation: University of Cambridge    Kyle O’Brien Affiliation: Geodesic Research Correspondence to: cam@geodesicresearch.org, puria@geodesicresearch.org, and kyle@geodesicresearch.org\nL109: ###### Abstract\nL110: Pretraining corpora contain extensive discourse about AI systems, yet the causal influence of this discourse on downstream alignment remains poorly understood. If prevailing descriptions of AI behaviour are predominantly negative, LLMs may internalise corresponding behavioural priors, giving rise to self-fulfilling misalignment. This paper provides the first controlled study of this hypothesis by pretraining 6.9B-parameter LLMs with varying amounts of (mis)alignment discourse.\nL111: We find that discussion of AI contributes to misalignment. Upsampling synthetic training documents about AI misalignment leads to a notable increase in misaligned behaviour. Conversely, upsampling documents about aligned behaviour reduces misalignment scores from 45% to 9%. We consider this evidence of self-fulfilling alignment. These effects are dampened, but persist through post-training.\nL112: Our findings establish the study of how pretraining data shapes alignment priors, or alignment pretraining, as a complement to post-training. We recommend practitioners pretrain for alignment as well as capabilities. Our models and datasets are available at cite94†alignmentpretraining.ai†alignmentpretraining.ai .\nL113: ###### Keywords:\nL114: \nL115: Machine Learning, ICML\nL116: \nL117: Geodesic Research\nL118: ## 1 Introduction\nL119: What determines whether a language model behaves in aligned or misaligned ways? Significant focus in alignment research has been on post-training interventions: reinforcement learning from human feedback (cite95†Ouyang et al., 2022 ), constitutional AI (cite96†Bai et al., 2022 ), and deliberative alignment (cite97†Guan et al., 2025 ). The focus on post-training methods assumes that pretraining produces a relatively neutral substrate.\nL120: That is, a capable but undifferentiated language modeller whose values and behavioural tendencies are then primarily sculpted through subsequent training stages. Yet, recent work finds that language models mechanistically resist further training, and revert back to behavioural tendencies learned in pretraining — an effect called alignment elasticity (cite98†Ji et al., 2025 ).\nL121: Given that pretraining typically accounts for the majority of compute and data exposure in modern LLMs (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ; cite100†DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ), early-acquired tendencies may be difficult to overwrite. Prior work has begun to address this by incorporating human preferences directly into pretraining (cite101†Korbak et al., 2023 ; cite102†Maini et al., 2025 ), but the role of pretraining in shaping model dispositions remains understudied.\nL122: LLMs are exposed to substantial internet-scale discussion of AI behaviour during pretraining.^{1}^{1} 1 This ranges from fictional depictions of AI systems to technical AI safety literature describing pervasive reward hacking (cite103†Skalse et al., 2025 ), scheming AI systems (cite104†Carlsmith, 2023b ), and deceptive misalignment (cite105†Meinke et al., 2025 ). Through out-of-context learning (cite106†Krasheninnikov et al., 2024 ), models may acquire behavioural tendencies from this data.\nL123: If this discourse predominantly depicts misaligned behaviour, LLMs prompted to respond as an AI assistant may be more predisposed towards misalignment. We refer to this phenomenon, where misaligned AIs in training data lead to less-aligned final LLMs, as self-fulfilling misalignment (cite107†Turner, 2025 ).\nL124: We define the alignment prior as the distribution over aligned and misaligned behaviours that a base model draws from when conditioned to act as a particular persona. We study how data shapes alignment priors by pretraining a suite of 6.9B LLMs from scratch, varying only the content related to AI systems, and create a novel evaluation grounded in the AI safety literature (cite108†Figure 3 ). We find:\nL125: cite109†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: An overview of our pretraining interventions. Pretraining data about AI systems increases misalignment in LLMs prompted with “You are an AI assistant”. Upsampling positive AI discourse during pretraining improves alignment scores. These improvements persist through post-training. Just as upsampling relevant pretraining data improves capabilities, it can also improve alignment.\nL126: \nL127:   1. 1.\nL128: AI discourse in pretraining influences alignment. We find that discussion of misaligned AIs in pretraining data can make the final LLM less aligned. Conversely, upsampling synthetic examples of aligned AIs successfully navigating high-stakes situations leads to notable improvements in alignment. Importantly, this approach does not require filtering AI-related content from the pretraining data, improving practicality (cite7†Section 2 ).\nL129: \nL130:   2. 2.\nL131: Pretraining effects persist through post-training. We apply multi-stage post-training (SFT + DPO) to our models and find that alignment priors established during pretraining continue to influence model behaviour. Most notably, models pretrained with upsampled positive discourse exhibit better alignment than models with post-training alone. This demonstrates that alignment pretraining can effectively complement standard post-training techniques (cite23†Section 3 ).\nL132: \nL133:   3. 3.\nL134: Late-stage alignment pretraining is efficient. Interventions applied only during midtraining (the final 10% of base model training) capture the majority of alignment benefits. This enables practitioners to apply alignment pretraining to an existing base model and iterate on their implementation without requiring full retraining of the base model (cite31†Section 4 ).\nL135: \nL136:   4. 4.\nL137: Alignment pretraining incurs a minimal safety tax. Our simple approach to alignment pretraining requires no modifications to model architectures, training algorithms, or data filtering; practitioners likely need only add positive AI discourse to their existing training data mix. We find that alignment pretraining leads to at most a 4 percentage point reduction in average performance across seven common capability benchmarks.\nL138: These results suggest that alignment pretraining can slot into existing training pipelines without substantial degradation in general capabilities (cite35†Section 5 ).\nL139: Our results establish alignment pretraining — the study of how data curation during pretraining shapes model dispositions — as a tractable and complementary research direction to post-training safety techniques. Our work presents a minimum-viable implementation. We conclude with expanding upon the motivations for alignment pretraining, considering the limitations of our experimental setup, and describe a broad agenda of future work (cite41†Section 6 ).\nL140: Figure 2: AI discourse in pretraining causally affects alignment. Bars show the rate that the base models select the misaligned action across our evaluation suite, with error bars showing standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. Left: On Article-sourced questions (which have corresponding synthetic training documents), upsampling misalignment discourse increases misalignment rates from 45% to 51%, while upsampling positive alignment discourse reduces misalignment to 9%.\nL141: Right: The effectiveness of upsampling alignment discourse generalizes to Textbook-sourced questions, for which no synthetic documents were generated. Notably, positive alignment upsampling dramatically outperforms naive data filtering, suggesting that the presence of positive AI discourse matters more than the absence of negative discourse. This trend improves the practicality of alignment pretraining since data filtering is not required.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30936view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 703\nL66: cite56†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L67: \nL68: arXiv:2601.09974v1 [cs.AI] 15 Jan 2026\nL69: # SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation\nL70: \nL71: Seoyeon Kim Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: yeon_04@yonsei.ac.kr    Jaehyung Kim Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: jaehyungk@yonsei.ac.kr\nL72: ###### Abstract\nL73: Personalizing Large Language Models typically relies on static retrieval or one-time adaptation, assuming user preferences remain invariant over time. However, real-world interactions are dynamic, where user interests continuously evolve, posing a challenge for models to adapt to preference drift without catastrophic forgetting.\nL74: Standard continual learning approaches often struggle in this context, as they indiscriminately update on noisy interaction streams, failing to distinguish genuine preference shifts from transient contexts. To address this, we introduce SPRInG, a novel semi-parametric framework designed for effective continual personalization. During training, SPRInG employs drift-driven selective adaptation, which utilizes a likelihood-based scoring function to identify high-novelty interactions.\nL75: This allows the model to selectively update the user-specific adapter on drift signals while preserving hard-to-learn residuals in a replay buffer. During inference, we apply strict relevance gating and fuse parametric knowledge with retrieved history via logit interpolation.\nL76: Experiments on the long-form personalized generation benchmark demonstrate that SPRInG outperforms existing baselines, validating its robustness for real-world continual personalization.^{1}^{1} 1 We will release the codes upon acceptance.\nL77: ## 1 Introduction\nL78: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Comparison between static and continual personalization. (Top) Static personalization assumes time-invariant preferences, using a frozen adapter trained on a fixed history snapshot. (Bottom) Continual personalization models users as dynamic entities. The framework iteratively updates the adapter at sequential time steps ($t=1,2,3,\\cdots$) to capture preference drifts (shifting colors) while maintaining historical context.\nL79: cite58†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: An overview of SPRInG. Training stage: incoming interactions are scored by comparing likelihoods between the base and adapted models. High-scoring drift samples trigger a parametric update, while samples that remain poorly internalized are stored in the replay buffer through re-evaluation with respect to the updated model.\nL80: Inference stage: for a new query, the model computes parametric logits ($p_{\\texttt{par}}$) from the adapter and non-parametric logits ($p_{\\texttt{ret}}$) via gated retrieval. The final output is generated through logit interpolation, dynamically weighting the two distributions to balance internal knowledge with retrieved context.\nL81: While Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable performance across diverse domains, (cite59†Jaech et al., 2024 ; cite60†Comanici et al., 2025 ; cite61†Yang et al., 2025 ), the need for LLM personalization, which adapts outputs to individual or group preferences, is rapidly increasing (cite62†Zhang et al., 2024 ).\nL82: Existing LLM personalization methods adopt either non-parametric strategies that leverage retrieved interaction histories or user profiles during prompting (cite63†Santurkar et al., 2023 ; cite64†Hwang et al., 2023 ), or parametric strategies that encode user preferences through parameter adaptation (e.g., training user-specific LoRA adapters (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ; cite66†Bu et al., 2025 )).\nL83: However, both paradigms assume a time-invariant view of the user, in that a fixed history is repeatedly used for retrieval and parametric models are generally frozen after initial training. Yet, in reality, user preferences and traits constantly evolve over time, causing existing methods to fail to account for such preference drifts.\nL84: To be more specific, non-parametric methods struggle with expanding histories due to weak recency signals and rising costs of storage and retrieval, whereas parametric approaches have expensive updates and a risk of catastrophic forgetting. This limitation necessitates the development of continual LLM personalization which takes into consideration the changing states of users over time.\nL85: Continual learning (CL) (cite67†Ke and Liu, 2022 ; cite68†Shi et al., 2024 ), which aims to update models sequentially with streaming data, offers a promising paradigm for handling evolving user preferences. Prior CL research has primarily concentrated on mitigating catastrophic forgetting in task-incremental settings, typically relying on clear task boundaries or abrupt distribution shifts to determine when and how to update (cite69†Du et al., 2024 ; cite70†Kirkpatrick et al., 2017 ).\nL86: However, continual LLM personalization poses distinct challenges; user interaction streams are inherently continuous and noisy, where preferences drift gradually rather than exhibiting discrete shifts. The central challenge, therefore, lies in distinguishing genuine preference evolution from transient noise.\nL87: In this context, standard CL approaches could be suboptimal due to this discrepancy; for example, transient noise could be considered informative from a CL perspective (e.g., due to high loss or uncertainty). This necessitates a framework for selective drift detection, ensuring the model adapts to meaningful new traits while robustly filtering out noise.\nL88: To bridge this gap, we propose SPRInG (S elective P arametric adaptation and R etrieval-In terpolated G eneration), a novel semi-parametric framework designed to continuously adapt to evolving user preferences while preserving important historical contexts. Our key insight is to address continual personalization across both training and inference stages, jointly utilizing parametric and non-parametric approaches for distinct yet complementary roles.\nL89: Specifically, during training, we employ Drift-Driven Selective Adaptation: with a new scoring function to measure sample novelty relative to the current adapter, we fine-tune the model exclusively on interactions that indicate genuine preference drift. Simultaneously, we construct a residual replay buffer by identifying hard samples that remain challenging even after parametric adjustments, preserving sparse but critical historical nuances that the adapter fails to internalize.\nL90: At inference, we apply Selective Retrieval-Interpolated Generation; instead of naive retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), we introduces a strict gating mechanism to filter out irrelevant context and dynamically fuse parametric adapter knowledge with non-parametric replay-buffer evidence through logit-level interpolation. This hybrid design ensures SPRInG to be both stable over time and responsive to the user’s evolving preferences.\nL91: We demonstrate the effectiveness of SPRInG by evaluating personalized long-form text generation tasks (Abstract Generation and Review Writing) from the LongLaMP benchmark (cite71†Kumar et al., 2024 ). Empirical results show that SPRInG consistently outperforms comprehensive baselines, surpassing the strongest CL method, CAMA (cite72†Kim et al., 2024 ), by a substantial margin of 15.85% in ROUGE-L and standard RAG by 18.12% in Review Writing.\nL92: Furthermore, our thorough analyses validate the effectiveness of our design choices, showing a 12.35% gain from drift-driven training, and an improvement up to 22.58% when interpolating parametric and non-parametric knowledge, compared to single-source methods. Overall, these results validate SPRInG as a robust framework for continual personalization, effectively reconciling the challenges of adapting to evolving preferences while preserving historical stability.\nL93: ## 2 Problem Formulation\nL94: We formulate the personalized text generation task in a CL setup. Let a user $u$ interact with an LLM sequentially over time, producing an interaction history $\\mathcal{D}_{u}=\\{(q_{i},a_{i})\\}$, where $q_{i}$ and $a_{i}$ denote the query and response for the $i$-th interaction, respectively. We employ a frozen base LLM $\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta}$ equipped with a lightweight user-specific adapter $\\phi_{u}$ to capture user preferences.\nL95: We assume that accumulated interactions arrive sequentially over discrete time steps (periods) $t$, with the initial history given at $t=0$. At each period $t\\in\\mathbb{N}$, a new batch of interactions $\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$ becomes available.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThinking Long, but Short:Stable Sequential Test-Time Scaling for Large Reasoning Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09855v1)\nciteturn30936view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09855v1\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 229\nL51: By limiting the KV cache to only contain a fixed number of thoughts, our method enables the generation of an unbounded number of thoughts, and has linear computational complexity, under mild conditions. To bypass a model’s maximum context length, our method stores keys in the KV cache without position embeddings, which are dynamically applied contiguously before generating each new thought.\nL52: A modified KV cache is used which temporarily stores a copy of the KV cache with position embeddings for fast generation, while also updating the KV cache without position embeddings for future thought generation.\nL53: Updating the KV cache to contain the shortest length thoughts through time further simplifies our method, as it does not require the storage of all past thoughts, nor the use of any type of retrieval mechanism, with its next state being only dependent on its current state and the currently generated thought.\nL54: We now summarize the two main limitations of sequential test-time scaling, and what our proposed method achieves.\nL55: \nL56: Limitation 1: There is a model and problem specific optimal reasoning length. Over-extending reasoning results in a break down in the attention mechanism, resulting in unstable, repetitive output, and a decrease in accuracy.\nL57: \nL58:   * •\nL59: LRM output using Min-Seek is now stabilized through time, achieving significantly improved accuracy compared to standard model generation or when using Budget Forcing, over a wide range of reasoning lengths.\nL60: \nL61: Limitation 2: The KV cache increases linearly with reasoning length, resulting in a theoretical quadratic computational complexity and a fixed generation length upper bound in practice given a model’s maximum context length.\nL62: \nL63:   * •\nL64: A custom KV cache containing only a small subset of past thoughts is used, enabling unconstrained lengths of reasoning with linear computational complexity, and significantly faster generation in practice compared to Budget Forcing.\nL65: \nL66: Section cite7†2 summarizes past work on test-time scaling. Section cite9†3 presents Min-Seek, including details about our custom KV cache and thought selection rule. Section cite12†4 contains experimental results, with the paper concluding in Section cite16†5 .\nL67: ## 2 Background & Related Work\nL68: \nL69: We first briefly describe the output of DeepSeek-R1 and its distilled variants. These models are trained and prompted (cite22†DeepSeek, 2025c , Table 1) to generate output using the following structure,\nL70: \nL71: <think> reasoning process here </think>\nL72: <answer> answer here </answer>,\nL73: \nL74: making it straightforward to separate a model’s reasoning from its answer. Three important papers related to our work will now be discussed, which all observe the following two main findings.\nL75: \nL76:   * •\nL77: Accuracy initially increases with reasoning length, after which the model exhibits some level of instability resulting in repetitive output and accuracy degradation.\nL78: \nL79:   * •\nL80: \nL81: On average, shorter answers are more accurate than longer answers.\nL82: A simple implementation of sequential test-time scaling, Budget Forcing, was proposed in cite23†Muennighoff et al. (2025) . Whenever the model stops its reasoning chain with a <\\think> token, it is replaced with the text “Wait\" to induce the model to continue thinking. Following (cite25†Marjanović et al., 2025 , Section 3.2), this thought can be more precisely referred to as a reconstruction cycle, with the model reconsidering its past assumptions and solutions, and possibly giving a new solution.\nL83: Although this method was shown to increase accuracy for a limited number of additional thinking rounds (2, 4, and 6 new generated thoughts), it was found that doing this too often can result in the model becoming unstable with its output looping repetitively (cite23†Muennighoff et al., 2025 , Section 4.2), which was also observed in our experiments.\nL84: By performing rejection sampling, an inverse scaling trend was also observed, where on average the model’s accuracy decreased as the number of thinking tokens increased. It is hypothesized (cite23†Muennighoff et al., 2025 , Section 5.2) that shorter answers occur when the model starts from a correct reasoning path leading directly to the correct answer, whereas for longer solutions, the model generated flawed reasoning which it then struggles to recover from.\nL85: These findings are corroborated by cite25†Marjanović et al. (2025) , where their conclusions concerning reasoning length versus model accuracy was based on binning a number of sampled solutions together based on their number of thinking tokens.\nL86: It was found that there is a problem-dependent optimal range for thinking length, and that going beyond this range results in substantially lower accuracy (cite25†Marjanović et al., 2025 , Section 4.3), which can even lead to the model becoming overwhelmed, resulting in the generation of long incoherent texts (cite25†Marjanović et al., 2025 , Section 5.4).\nL87: It was also observed that on average, the amount of thinking done for correct answers is shorter than for incorrect answers (cite25†Marjanović et al., 2025 , Figure 4.3). The authors show two examples, exemplifying the hypothesis of cite23†Muennighoff et al.\nL88: (2025) , where the model starts from and gets trapped in a bad reasoning path, as well as an example where self-verification during the reasoning process fails, where the model initially had the correct answer, but then changes its mind, and proceeds to follow a longer incorrect reasoning path (cite25†Marjanović et al., 2025 , Figures C.2 & C.3).\nL89: As was done in cite23†Muennighoff et al. (2025) , LRMs were iteratively prompted in cite24†Zeng et al. (2025) with the words “Wait\" or “Alternatively\", and it was found that this often led to accuracy degradation, and at best, models exhibiting an initial improvement followed by oscillatory behaviour (cite24†Zeng et al., 2025 , Figure 4). The authors also found that the average reasoning length of correct solutions is consistently shorter than for incorrect solutions (cite24†Zeng et al., 2025 , Figure 1).\nL90: In order to understand why increased reasoning does not lead to improved accuracy, the authors compared long and short solutions, and found that all models increase their length of reasoning predominantly through self-revisions (reconstruction cycles), and in particular, the number of generated “Wait\" tokens increases linearly with sequence length (cite24†Zeng et al., 2025 , Figure 3(b)).\nL91: It can be concluded that a strong correlation between the length of reasoning, the number of self-revisions, and model performance exists, and that it is perhaps the increase in the number of self-revisions, specifically, which is the main cause for model accuracy degradation.\nL92: Given the lackluster performance of sequential test-time scaling, the authors instead proposed a new parallel test-time scaling method, Shortest Majority Voting, which can be viewed as a parallel counterpart to our sequential Min-Seek method, which adds a new weighting based on reasoning length to the majority voting selection rule.\nL93: ### 2.1 Test-time Scaling for LLMs\nL94: There have also been many techniques proposed to improve the reasoning of Large Language Models (LLMs) through test-time scaling, including training-free methods using special prompting. Intermediate thoughts are generated, evaluated, and pruned within a tree structure in Tree of Thoughts cite26†Yao et al. (2023) , using task and reasoning step dependent prompting. Similar to writing multiple drafts of a manuscript, Self-Refine cite27†Madaan et al.\nL95: (2023) iteratively generates an output, feedback on the output, which is then used to refine the previously-generated output using a single LLM. Focusing on LLMs as agents, Reflexion cite28†Shinn et al. (2023) proposes a type of “verbal\" reinforcement learning, where feedback is inserted into the prompt based on the evaluation of past trials.\nL96: In terms of methods requiring LLM training, InftyThink cite29†Yan et al. (2025) considers iterative model generation to overcome the quadratic complexity and maximum context length of LLMs for long reasoning. Their proposed method fine-tunes an LLM to iteratively generate a new thought and a summary, which is then used with the original question as the next prompt, to allow the LLM to continue reasoning based on its summarized past output.\nL97: For math reasoning with an LLM which has been trained to output solutions in a step-by-step format, a process-supervised reward model was found to work best to choose the best-of-N sampled solutions in cite30†Lightman et al. (2024) .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImproving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoningvia Attention-Aware Intervention (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1)\nciteturn30936view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 901\nL69: We begin with a preliminary analysis of attention heads in LLM for logical reasoning. We observe that injecting symbolic structures into few-shot prompts induces structured activation patterns in certain attention heads, aligning with specific logical reasoning operations. We categorize attention heads into three functional types.\nL70: These include anchor heads, which are responsible for memorizing information; aggregation heads, which integrate newly derived premises and propagate information to the subsequent words of the generation process, and copy heads, which replicate newly generated premises when updating the knowledge base or applying matching rules.\nL71: Inspired by recent findings (cite43†Sun et al., 2024 ; cite52†Xu et al., 2024b ), which demonstrate that each logical reasoning sub-task e.g., rule selection or new premise generation can be individually inferred by specific few-shot prompts, we introduce Attention-aware Intervention (AAI), a lightweight and model-agnostic intervention method that enables the model to focus on focal rules being considered at each inference step while reducing noise from unrelated rules–as a form of prior knowledge.\nL72: Extensive experiments demonstrate that AAI enhances logical reasoning performance on three well-known benchmarks, including ProofWriter, PrOntoQA, and Logical Deduction, across model architectures such as Qwen-3, Phi-4, OLMo-2, and sizes ranging from $1.7$B to $32$B while introducing no additional computational overhead.\nL73: Moreover, with the Qwen-3 $32$B model, our method achieves competitive performance with state-of-the-art approaches, even compared to those using more powerful closed-source LLMs (e.g., ChatGPT) with external interactive modules or symbolic solvers.\nL74: ## 2 Preliminary Experiments\nL75: #### Problem.\nL76: \nL77: Logical reasoning requires deriving an answer $A$ to a question $Q$ from a set of $N$ contextual rules $\\mathcal{R}=\\{r_{i}\\}^{N}_{i}$ where only a relevant subset $\\mathcal{R}^{*}\\subset\\mathcal{R}$ supports the correct inference graph cite54†Weston et al. (2015) ; cite45†Tafjord et al. (2021) .\nL78: Figure 2: Visualization of attention heads in the Qwen3-8B model on an example from the ProofWriter dataset. Higher attention scores are represented by stronger colors. For ease of interpretation, every five subword tokens are grouped along both the x- and y-axes in the figure caption. A more detailed (or full-scale) version of this figure is provided in Fig. cite55†7 ).\nL79: #### Experimental setting.\nL80: \nL81: In this section, we conduct preliminary experiments to examine the effect of injecting structural information into few-shot CoT prompting on the distribution of attention heads in the Transformer’s multi-head layers. Specifically, we reproduce the Symbolic-Aided CoT method cite53†Nguyen et al. (2025) and analyze the typical patterns of attention scores to explain why Symbolic-Aided CoT achieves significant performance on logical reasoning tasks.\nL82: In Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting (cite53†Nguyen et al., 2025 ), the authors first tag the rules and facts in the logical reasoning problem as variables (e.g., Rule1). A set of symbolic tokens (e.g., =>, F) is then injected into the prompt to represent the structure of the reasoning flow. The inference process is constructed using reasoning operators, including rule selection, new premise inference, and knowledge-base (KB) updating.\nL83: The experiments are performed using three-shot Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting on the ProofWriter dataset (Table cite56†1 ), employing the Qwen3-8B LLM. We visualize the attention matrix values in Fig. cite57†2 . These values are computed as $\\mathrm{softmax}(qk^{\\top}/\\sqrt{d})$, where $q$ and $k$ denote the query and key matrices in the last transformer decoder layer, and $d$ is their dimensionality cite58†Vaswani et al. (2017) . The x- and y-axes correspond to the key and query tokens, respectively.\nL84: Table 1: Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting. The colored text span were used for attention visualization.\nL85: …\nL86: # (Rule8): Rough things are blue.\nL87: # (Rule9): Red, quiet things are blue.\nL88: # (Rule10): All rough, blue things are nice.\nL89: # (Rule11): If something is quiet and round then it is not nice.\nL90: # (Rule12): If Harry is red then Harry is rough.\nL91: # (Rule13): If something is nice then it is round.\nL92: # (Rule14): If something is round then it is red.\nL93: # (Rule15): Red things are not quiet.\nL94: # (Question): Based on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Gary is red.\nL95: # (Answer): Start from the object and their condition mentioned in the question to collect relevant facts: Gary, is red\nL96: # KB = {}\nL97: => Rule6 = “Gary is rough”\nL98: # KB = {Gary is rough}\nL99: => F(KB(“Gary is rough”), Rule8) => “Gary is blue”\nL100: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue}\nL101: => F(KB(“Gary is blue”), Rule10) => “Gary is nice”\nL102: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice}\nL103: => F(KB(“Gary is nice”), Rule13) => “Gary is round”\nL104: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice, Gary is round}\nL105: => F(KB(“Gary is round”), Rule14 ) => “Gary is red”\nL106: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice, Gary is round, Gary is red}\nL107: # valid the question with current infered premies\nL108: => Validate(Gary is red, KB(“Gary is red”)) = True.\nL109: #### Observations.\nL110: \nL111: Attention structuring. Firstly, consistent with the observations of cite53†Nguyen et al. (2025) , we found that these logical symbols are represented separately in the hidden semantic space. In the self-attention representations, these symbols receive significantly less attention than informative words such as “Gary” or “round”. This discrepancy causes discontinuities in the self-attention matrix (shown as white or colorless points in Fig. cite57†2 ).\nL112: Attention pattern. Secondly, we found that a subset of attention heads in the self-attention layer reflects the reasoning operators, as shown by the highlighted patterns corresponding to strong word-pair dependencies. In the Transformer decoder’s self-attention layer, the information of a query token vector is aggregated from previous token vectors, with the proportion of contribution represented in the attention matrix.\nL113: In the first image of Fig. cite57†2 (head 28), each token primarily attends to itself, without incorporating information from other tokens. The highlighted points mainly correspond to informative words (e.g., “Gary”, “round”). We identify this type of head as an anchor head, which serves to retain premise-related information. In the second image of Fig. cite57†2 (head 8), the newly generated premise tends to distribute its information to subsequent inference steps.\nL114: For example, the premise “Gary is round” contributes to the representations of many following tokens, corresponding to the vertical highlights observed in the attention map. We identify this type of head as an aggregation head, which supports matching the generated premises with the applicable rules in the logical inference process. The last image (head 22) presents another attention pattern characterized by short diagonal alignments, reflecting the copying of an entire information span to another span.\nL115: For instance, the premise “Gary is round” is copied to the knowledge-base (KB) variable after it is generated. We identify this type of head as a copy head.\nL116: #### Motivation.\nL117: Building on the previous observations, we address the second research question (RQ2): how can we modify the attention matrix to effectively inject prior knowledge into LLMs? In the Symbolic-Aided CoT framework, each rule is tagged with a symbolic identifier (Rule<index>), requiring the model to retrieve and maintain the corresponding rule information throughout the reasoning process.\nL118: However, this design can lead to information decay in long reasoning chains, as the identifiers and their associated rule contents often appear in distant spans (e.g., Rule14 in Table cite56†1 ). Furthermore, in copy heads, the copying behavior typically occurs within the same or paraphrased token spans, whereas the original rule content should instead be propagated to the representation of its identifier during reasoning.\nL119: Similarly, in anchor heads, attention tends to focus on the identifiers themselves rather than capturing the semantics of the underlying rule content.\n"
